<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wallis Island</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wallis Island, called Uvea by its inhabitants, is the main island of the French overseas collectivity of Wallis and Futuna in the south-west Pacific. Surrounded by a lagoon and small islets, it holds the territory's capital, Mata-Utu. Settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesians">Polynesians</a>, it was conquered by Tonga in the 15th century and is named after the British navigator Samuel Wallis, who sighted it in 1767.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wallis-island.jpg" alt="Wallis Island" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thommy · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Uvea.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Wallis, called ʻUvea in Wallisian, is a Polynesian island and atoll in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a> belonging to the French overseas collectivity of Wallis and Futuna. It lies north of Tonga, northeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a> and west of Samoa, and is of volcanic origin, with fertile soil that allows subsistence farming. Its highest point is Mount Lulu Fakahega, 131 metres. Several crater lakes, among them Lalolalo, are almost perfect circles with straight vertical walls.</p><p>Together with some fifteen smaller islands on a large barrier reef, Wallis forms the Wallis archipelago, 240 kilometres northeast of Futuna and Alofi. The climate is hot and humid, with an average temperature around 26 °C and a rainy season from November to April, which is also the season of tropical cyclones. The island is divided into three districts: Hihifo in the north, Hahake and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mua-tonga">Muʻa</a> in the south.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Archaeological excavations have identified sites on Wallis dating from about 1400. The island was part of the Tongan maritime empire, but the influence of the Tuʻi Tonga declined until ʻUvea became important in itself. Several high-ranking Tongan titles, such as Halaevalu, trace their descent from ʻUvea, and the large canoe Lomipeau, according to legend, was built there as a gift to the Tuʻi Tonga. The fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talietumu">Talietumu</a>, near Lotoalahi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mua-tonga">Muʻa</a>, was the last holdout of the Tongans until they were defeated.</p><p>The island was named after Captain Samuel Wallis, who saw it on 16 August 1767 from HMS Dolphin, after he had found Tahiti. In 1835 armed missionaries from Tonga attacked, trying to convert the island to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a> by force. In October 1837 Jean-Baptiste Pompallier visited and left a priest and a brother, and four years later Wallis was reported to be a Catholic community. On 5 April 1842 its authorities requested French protection, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> treaty was signed in April 1887.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In 1942 the island's administration, pro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> until then, was deposed on 26 May by a Free French corvette from New Caledonia, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-marine-corps">US Marine Corps</a> units landed on 29 May. After a referendum in 1959, Wallis became a French overseas territory in 1961. The population was 8,333 in 2018, about 72 per cent of the territory, and most people speak ʻUvean as their mother tongue. Almost all are Roman Catholic, and the chief church is the Cathedral of Mata Utu, the capital.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wallis (island)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Wallis Island</b>, called ʻUvea by its people, was settled by Polynesians around 1000 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-uvea">Kingdom of Uvea</a></li></ul></section>
